sections.body:
In 2014 Amazon engineers described a bug in DynamoDB's replication and group-membership protocol that could lose data. It needed a particular sequence of failures and recovery steps interleaved with ordinary processing, and the shortest sequence that showed it was 35 high-level steps long. It had passed extensive design reviews, code reviews and testing.

Most distributed bugs have that shape. The code works in nearly every run and fails only in a few schedules: a message delayed just past a timeout, a pause that starts just after a write is applied and ends just after a failover. An example-based test runs one schedule, whichever one your laptop, the JVM and the network happened to produce that time. Run it 500 times in CI and you have sampled 500 schedules near the common case. If one of them fails, you can't run that schedule again.

Partitions deserve a special mention, because injecting them is cheap and few teams do it. Alquraan and colleagues (OSDI 2018) studied 136 failures caused by network partitions in 25 widely used distributed systems. Most were catastrophic (lost data, deleted data coming back, broken locks), 88% could be triggered by isolating a single node, and 62% were deterministic: once the partition happened, the failure followed. Their tool, NEAT, found 32 new failures in seven systems.

So testing a distributed system means searching schedules and faults on purpose. The industry has settled on layers, each answering a different question (the figure):

- model checkers such as TLA+ and P check the design, in every schedule of a small model;
- deterministic simulation checks the real code, in millions of seeded runs you can replay;
- Jepsen-style testing checks the deployed system under real faults, judged only by what clients saw;
- property-based tests check one component against its invariants on generated inputs;
- chaos experiments check production, with its alerts, runbooks and people.

The rest of this lesson takes them in turn and says what each one misses.

steps.code:
private val rng = Random(seed)
private val drawn = Fault.random(rng)       // always drawn: keeps the stream of numbers stable
val fault: Fault = fault ?: drawn

private class Event(val at: Long, val seq: Long, val node: String?, val action: () -> Unit)
private val queue = PriorityQueue(compareBy<Event>({ it.at }, { it.seq }))
private fun schedule(at: Long, node: String?, action: () -> Unit) { queue += Event(at, seq++, node, action) }

fun search(seeds: LongRange, protocol: Protocol): Run? {
    for (seed in seeds) {
        val run = Simulation(seed, protocol).run()
        if (run.violation != null) return run      // the seed is the bug report
    }
    return null
}

answer.src:
while (queue.isNotEmpty() && violation == null) {
    val e = queue.poll()                         // smallest (at, seq) first
    if (e.at > endAt) break
    now = e.at                                   // virtual time jumps; nothing sleeps
    if (e.node != null && e.node in down) continue
    val wake = e.node?.let { pausedUntil[it] }
    if (wake != null && now < wake) {            // paused: same event, later, order kept by seq
        schedule(wake, e.node, e.action)
        continue
    }
    e.action()                                   // may send messages, set timers
    check()                                      // the invariant, after every event
}

// Two runs of one seed differ if anything bypasses the seed: Random.Default or
// System.nanoTime() in the code under test, iteration over a HashSet of objects
// with identity hash codes, or a real thread racing the event loop.
